{
	title: 'Noninterrupting /plugin-sync review session and discrepancy indicator',
	spec: `Each /sync opens a fresh scripted hal/sync session for the invoking terminal's client/host pair in place of its previous review tab (closed, kept on disk), so every review starts with no history, using no provider or tokens. Startup comparison (task zh7) never switches focus or opens a modal: normal startup lands where the user expects. A persistent compact terminal status indicator says Plugins differ (N) — /plugin-sync until discrepancies are resolved or ignored. Announce newly discovered discrepancies through the existing notice line/stack without permanently crowding out errors. Reconnection or repeated detection does not duplicate notices, sessions or questions. Opening the review is an explicit user action; subsequent changes update it without stealing focus. Existing history and question mechanisms render the scripted session; no new dialog framework.

For each portable file both computers have and that differs, Hal merges three-way from the nearest common version (git merge-file; config.ason one setting at a time, task w0h) (an empty one when the copies share no history) and says in one line how they relate: <client> has changes to <file>; <host> can fast-forward (or the reverse); Both have changes to <file>; they merge cleanly; or <client> and <host> have incompatible changes to <file> in N places; choose how to resolve. Then each side as its name and role (<client hostname without domain> (this computer); <host> (server)) and when it changed, in the shared timestamp format. Each copy choice previews the changes it keeps since the version both share, as a combined diff (git diff -c of that version against both copies, signs flipped; fence language combined-diff), each change in the colors of the computer that made it: green + is what <client> added, red - what it deleted, blue + what <host> added, orange - what it deleted (diff add/remove and add2/remove2 colors). Taking one computer's copy discards the other's changes: the outcome says so, and the preview shows them undone in that computer's column and colors, its additions as - rows (legend: <name>'s addition, discarded) and its deletions as + rows (<name>'s deletion, undone). Use both changes and I merged it myself show both computers' changes; Use <client>'s copy only <client>'s, Use <host>'s copy only <host>'s, with one line of context around each change. A line both computers changed shows both marks, each in its color. A legend above the diff names each mark it shows, in its color (+  <client> added, -  <client> deleted,  + <host> added,  - <host> deleted); absent marks are left out. The user asked for these colors: previews whose marks meant gets and loses read as who made the change. Without conflicts Use both changes (or Use <side>'s changes when only one side changed) is offered first and preselected; with conflicts, I merged it myself, and both copies are written beside the file (<file>.local, <file>.server); I merged it myself sends the file as it is then, and settling removes the copies. Then Use <host>'s copy and Use <client>'s copy, each omitted when it equals the result. Every question ends with Not now (skips the file until the next /sync; Escape answers it, hint Escape: skip question) and Never sync <file> on <client> (the client's .syncignore). When nothing is left, the review names the files the server never syncs and asks whether to sync again any file this client never syncs (Done ends it), so that choice stays visible and reversible. Show no version ids or ancestry jargon. When only one side has it, show no diff: say which side has it and whether the other deleted it or never had it, offer Copy to <host> or Copy here and Delete here or Delete on <host>, and preselect the copy. Each option carries a one-sentence outcome (form choice outcomes). Missing and empty files remain distinct. Not now writes, deletes and ignores nothing; the discrepancy remains discoverable. Never sync uses task zh7's client-side .syncignore policy. Recheck versions when answered and show a revised comparison if either side changed. Report success only after the destination acknowledges the applied update; lifecycle notices follow task b66. Disconnected endpoints cannot complete a pending transfer; show why and allow review after reconnect.

This is a terminal feature. Web gets a discrepancy indicator only if existing shared code supplies it with zero additional work; add no web-specific UI, features or exceptions. Keep config sync and automation deferred. Demonstrate the end-to-end flow with portable keybinding overrides and two different shipped theme selections (task 4c1), including keeping them separate; do not commit real user settings. Verify no startup focus theft with many tabs, discoverability from another tab, Esc deferral, repeat-command focus, ignored-file removal from the count and stale-answer refresh.`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		"Wiring: /plugin-sync is a host command, not clientOnly, because sessions, forms, tabs and focus (the go event) live on the host; the remote terminal owns only the comparison and file writes. host/plugin-sync-session.ts finds the typing connection through clients.state.senders and its home through pluginSync.state.followers (now a Map of client to the home its inventory named), opens or reopens one tab per client home named Plugin sync <first 6 hex of the home id> with model hal/plugin-sync, and starts its turn unless a turn or question is under way. The scripted model relays each turn to that home's connected terminal as a plugin-sync event { review: session, answers? }; the terminal (host/plugin-sync-review.ts, wired by main.remote) applies the answer, recompares and replies with a plugin-sync step command { session, say, ask? } checked by common/plugin-sync.ts (forms.invalid). The step is said at once, not typed out: a diff is too long to pace.",
		"turns.runTurn reads a scripted reply's ask, pause and after when its round ends rather than when the model is called, so a stream may settle them while it runs; the relay sets ask after the terminal answers.",
		"Escape pauses the host turn and closes the question, so the terminal hears nothing and writes nothing. A relay wait ends on Escape (the turn's abort signal) or, with the reason said, when no terminal of that home is connected at a checkMs (5 s) check. Session bindings are host memory: after a host restart /plugin-sync binds the tab again. The terminal remembers per session the file and version ids it last asked about; after a terminal restart an answer is not applied and the current comparison is asked instead. Without a later timestamp, Keep separate is preselected: it changes neither plugin file.",
		'Indicator: appView.state.plugins, shown by status-row as the right group after advisories in the warning color, so it is dropped last after them. Notices: one per newly seen (file, client id, server id), key plugin-sync, so reconnects and repeated comparisons add none. The web shows no indicator: no shared code supplies one.',
		'Invariant: a file present on one side only is asked as copy or delete with no diff, and deletion is never preselected. An all-plus diff with "Use server version" preselected deleted a plugin on review (restored from history).',
		'Superseded notes: Escape now skips (form skip; the restarted turn arrives with no answer while a question is remembered, so the terminal answers Not now) and Not now, not Keep separate, is the fallback preselection. The offline version flag (gev) was dropped: nothing but a display label read it. Timestamps use common/titles.ts time(), shared with card headers.',
		'config.ason is compared in sorted key order (config-sync view), so a setting that only moved shows no diff. The sync question has no note about non-portable settings: they never change, so mentioning them only confused.',
		'Each /sync replaces the review tab with a new session (user: a reused tab showed a long transcript of old reviews and stale warnings). This supersedes the wiring note: no tab is focused or reopened.',
		'Simplest first (user): one git diff -c view replaced per-choice diffs and the config.ason per-setting merge. git diff --cc was rejected: it hides hunks where the result equals one side, so a one-sided change would not show. Known cost: adjacent insertions (common in sorted config.ason) and copies without shared history conflict.',
		'Bug fix invariant: /sync typed in the review tab itself follows to its replacement. The host sends the go before closing the old tab; a terminal whose tab closes first moves to a neighbor and ignores the go.',
		'Bug fix invariant: combining config.ason keeps a comment changed on one side only. combine built its result into the parsed client object, overwriting the client comments it then compared, so the client edited comments were silently lost.',
		'Bug fix invariant: a sync question starts on an option it offers. A fast-forward offered no merge, yet initial named merge; findIndex gave -1 and the terminal crashed in forms.start.'
	],
